WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.840
Hello, I'm Dr. White.

00:01.840 --> 00:03.360
Welcome to the FAMU College

00:03.360 --> 00:06.020
of Education Action Research Learning Lab.

00:06.020 --> 00:08.520
This is a practice space for undergraduate and graduate

00:08.520 --> 00:12.120
students who want to understand how action research works

00:12.120 --> 00:14.920
before conducting any real inquiry.

00:14.920 --> 00:18.600
Think of it as a flight simulator for practitioner research.

00:18.600 --> 00:20.320
You will make authentic decisions,

00:20.320 --> 00:24.520
but every classroom participant and data point is fictional.

00:24.520 --> 00:27.700
Begin by choosing the right research space.

00:27.700 --> 00:29.480
Undergraduate and graduate students

00:29.480 --> 00:32.960
may use the lab to practice with its fictional cases.

00:32.960 --> 00:35.380
If you have real participants or data,

00:35.380 --> 00:36.880
or if you intend to use the result

00:36.880 --> 00:41.600
for a thesis, dissertation, IRB application, publication,

00:41.600 --> 00:43.780
or other original research,

00:43.780 --> 00:45.280
the lab will stop and direct you

00:45.280 --> 00:47.840
to faculty-approved research support.

00:47.840 --> 00:50.360
Do not enter names, upload records,

00:50.360 --> 00:53.620
or use the simulation to produce real research.

00:53.620 --> 00:56.680
Next, choose one of three fictional cases.

00:56.680 --> 01:00.400
Algebraic reasoning, expressive reading fluency,

01:00.400 --> 01:03.400
or equitable participation in science discussions.

01:03.400 --> 01:06.300
Each case presents a focus problem of practice,

01:06.300 --> 01:10.720
a manageable time frame, and entirely synthetic evidence.

01:10.720 --> 01:13.520
Choose the context that interests you.

01:13.520 --> 01:17.320
The same action research cycle works across all three.

01:17.320 --> 01:20.600
Now you begin thinking like a teacher-researcher.

01:20.600 --> 01:22.400
First, identify an observable problem

01:22.400 --> 01:25.400
without blaming learners or assuming a cause.

01:25.400 --> 01:28.400
Then select a focused inquiry question,

01:28.400 --> 01:31.800
a strong question names the learners,

01:31.800 --> 01:33.440
the instructional action,

01:33.440 --> 01:36.240
the change you will examine, and the time frame.

01:36.240 --> 01:38.320
Next, choose one manageable action

01:38.320 --> 01:40.960
that directly matches the question.

01:40.960 --> 01:43.400
Action research is stronger when one source

01:43.400 --> 01:46.120
does not carry the entire conclusion.

01:46.120 --> 01:48.720
In the evidence step, select a balanced set

01:48.720 --> 01:51.760
that combines learner performance, learner perspective,

01:51.760 --> 01:53.200
and teacher observation.

01:53.200 --> 01:55.600
This is called triangulation.

01:55.600 --> 01:59.240
Make your own choice before opening the Coach’s Lens.

01:59.240 --> 02:01.440
The coach helps you compare reasoning

02:01.440 --> 02:03.800
that does not make the decision for you.

02:03.800 --> 02:07.360
The Analyze screen presents synthetic rubric scores,

02:07.360 --> 02:11.160
confidence results, and a fictional learner comment.

02:11.160 --> 02:12.880
Look for patterns across sources,

02:12.880 --> 02:15.920
but avoid saying the intervention proved a cause.

02:15.920 --> 02:18.760
A short classroom cycle can reveal a promising pattern

02:18.760 --> 02:20.840
and raise better questions.

02:20.840 --> 02:24.120
Write what changed, what remains uncertain,

02:24.120 --> 02:27.120
and one alternative explanation.

02:27.120 --> 02:30.600
Your own words become part of the final learning report.

02:30.600 --> 02:33.440
Action research does not end with one result.

02:33.440 --> 02:36.720
Decide what you would keep, what you would adjust,

02:36.720 --> 02:39.600
and what you would examine in a second cycle.

02:39.600 --> 02:41.840
This is the heart of the process.

02:41.840 --> 02:46.720
Plan, act, observe, reflect, and plan again.

02:46.720 --> 02:48.960
The goal is not to prove that you were right.

02:48.960 --> 02:52.280
The goal is to improve practice through disciplined learning.

02:52.280 --> 02:55.440
At the end, the learning lab assembles your decisions,

02:55.440 --> 02:58.000
interpretation, reflection, and next cycle plan

02:58.000 --> 03:00.520
into an action research learning report.

03:00.520 --> 03:03.760
The report is permanently marked instructional simulation,

03:03.760 --> 03:06.000
not original research.

03:06.000 --> 03:08.240
You may print it as evidence of learning,

03:08.240 --> 03:10.080
but it is not a research paper

03:10.080 --> 03:12.960
and should not be submitted as original research.

03:12.960 --> 03:15.080
When you are ready, select “Enter the Learning Lab”,

03:15.080 --> 03:17.080
choose a case and begin the cycle.
